      "side": 1,
      "message": "Not in this chain. I was going to explain more in the upcoming Chromium CL that will make all of the shortcuts use this OnKeyDown path on MacOS.\n\nBasically it works like this:\n\n 1. There\u0027s an objective-C++ plaform native event handler that checks for these shortcuts https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:chrome/browser/ui/cocoa/accelerators_cocoa.mm;l\u003d36-37,46,82-90;drc\u003d0bf31feb3731abd3c1e09456575afd8ebaba6305\n 2. That becomes a mojo message and ends up here in C++ which calls ExecuteEditCommand(\"Undo\") etc https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/platform/widget/input/frame_widget_input_handler_impl.cc;l\u003d206;drc\u003de7c21c2a4b1c1b81de83b6a4a12375c055ae6e8a\n 3. That ends up in the PdfWebViewPlugin here https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:pdf/pdf_view_web_plugin.cc;l\u003d930;drc\u003d73673cf4ab0c6ea4844aa6f545f90db0726e23cf\n \nSo basically when PDFium returns true from OnKeyDown it blocks that platform-specific event handler and PdfViewWebPlugin never gets the ExecuteEditCommand. That native event handler is necessary flash the edit menu.",
